An ant has no single purpose, but it has many functions: aerating soil, dispersing seeds, recycling nutrients, and sustaining the colony of which it is a part. Yet to speak of *purpose* is to assume an intention behind its existence, and nature offers none—evolution shaped the ant not to fulfill a role, but simply to survive and reproduce. Perhaps purpose is a category we project onto the world rather than find in it. The ant, untroubled by such questions, simply lives according to its nature—and in that, there may be a lesson for us.
